#import "defs.typ": *

=== Construction and enumeration of subgroups
<sec:l1996-enumeration-construction>

#theorem[
  The number $d(G,K)$ of normal $D$-invariant subgroups of the classic-type
  group $U(G,K)$ over a field $K$ of order $> 2$ is equal to:
  $ 1/n binom(2n, n-1) quad "for" G = A_(n-1), n > 1; $
  $ binom(n, floor(n/2)) quad "for" G = ""^2 A_(n-1), n > 1; $
  $
    binom(2n, n) quad "for" G = ""^2 D_(n+1), n >= 2, abs(K) > 4, "or"
    K = 2K, G = B_n, "or" C_n, n >= 1;
  $
  #source(3, printed: 51)
  $ binom(2n-1, n) + 4^(n-1) quad "for" 2K = 0, G = B_n "or" C_n, n > 2; $
  $ binom(2n-1, n) + binom(2n-2, n) quad "for" G = D_n, n > 2. $
] <th:l1996-enumeration-normal-count>

We want to describe normal $D$-invariant subgroups of $U G(K)$ under the
assumptions on $G$ and $K$ in Theorem~@th:l1996-enumeration-normal-count.

In the sequel, we suppose that the roots $r,s in Phi$ satisfy the inequality
$s <= r$ if $r-s$ is a linear combination of fundamental roots with non-negative
coefficients. The roots $r$ and $s$ are called _incident_ if $r <= s$ or
$s <= r$. The partial order $<=$ is also defined on the system $""^2 Phi$
composed of minimal nonempty subsets invariant under proper symmetry
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1982, Lemma~@lem:l1982-parabolic-root-folding].

For a set of pairwise nonincident roots $r_1,r_2,dots,r_m in G$, we select a
subgroup $T(r_1,dots,r_m)$. By definition, it is generated by all possible root
subgroups corresponding to the roots $s$ such that $r_i <= s$, even in the case
of a single $i$.

#lemma[
  Subgroups $T(r_1,dots,r_m)$ of the classic type group $U G(K) ≅ N G(K)$ are
  different for different sets of pairwise nonincident elements
  $r_1,r_2,dots,r_m in G$. All these subgroups are normal $D$-invariant
  subgroups and exhaust them if $abs(K) > 2$, with the additional conditions
  $abs(K) > 4$ for $G = ""^2 D_n$, $n >= 3$, and $2K = K$ for $G = B_n$ and
  $C_n$.
] <lem:l1996-enumeration-root-subgroups>

#proof[
  We only need to prove the last statement of the lemma. Under the stated field
  conditions, the commutant of two root subgroups in $U G(K)$ is generated by
  root subgroups. Under the same conditions, any normal $D$-invariant subgroup
  is also generated by root subgroups (see [@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990,
  Section~@sec:l1990-chevalley-characteristic-subgroups;
  @bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1992,
  Theorem~@th:l1992-chevalley-classical-ideals;
  @bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1982,
  Theorem~@th:l1982-parabolic-normal-basis-decomposition]). Let $2K = K$ for
  $G = B_n$ or $C_n$. By Lemmas~@lem:l1990-chevalley-classical-commutators to
  @lem:l1990-chevalley-twisted-a-relations
  of [@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990], the normal closure of the $r$-root
  subgroup in $U G(K)$ ∋ all the $s$-root subgroups for $r <= s$ and is
  generated by them. Lemma~@lem:l1996-enumeration-root-subgroups is proved.
]

#import "diagrams/stepline.typ": stepline
#drawing(stepline) <fig:l1996-enumeration-stepline>

#source(4, printed: 52)Below we use a representation of $U G(K)$ in terms of
$B_n^+$-, $C_n^+$-, and $B C_n^+$-matrices (see
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990]) and the notation (see
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Bourbaki1968, Tabl. I–IV])
$
  epsilon_i - v epsilon_j = p_(i,v j), quad 1 <= j <= i <= n,
  v in {0,-1,1}.
$
If $r_1 = p_(i_1,j_1), dots, r_m = p_(i_m,j_m)$, then the task of enumeration of
subgroups $T(r_1,dots,r_m)$ turns out to be equivalent (under the restrictions
on $G$ and $K$ made in Lemma~@lem:l1996-enumeration-root-subgroups) to that of
enumeration of the steplines $L$ in corresponding $Phi^+$-matrix (see
Fig.~@fig:l1996-enumeration-stepline). Hence the numbers
$
  d(""^2 A_(2n-1),K), quad d(B_n,K) quad (2K = K), quad
  d(C_n,K) quad (2K = K), quad d(""^2 D_(n+1),K)
$
coincide. On the other hand, it turns out that $d(B_n,K)$ is equal to the number
of integer-pair sequences $(i_1,j_1),(i_2,j_2),dots,(i_m,j_m)$ $(m >= 0)$ if
$-i_l < j_l < i_l$ for all $l$ and $j_1 < j_2 < dots < j_m$,
$i_1 < i_2 < dots < i_m <= n$.

The subgroup $T(r_1,dots,r_m)$ of the adjoint group
$N Phi lr((K)) ≅ U Phi lr((K))$ of normal type is also a subalgebra of the Lie
ring $N Phi lr((K))$. Its basis is formed by elements $e_s$ of Chevalley basis
with $r_i <= s$, even in the case of single $i$. By
Lemma~@lem:l1990-chevalley-classical-structure-signs of
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990] the subalgebras $T(r_1,dots,r_m)$ are
$D$-invariant ideals of $N Phi lr((K))$.

Consider the auxiliary table
$
  mat(

    , , , a_(0,0), , , ;
    , , a_(1,-1), a_(1,0), a_(1,1), , ;
    , , dots, dots, dots, , ;
    a_(n,-n), dots, a_(n,-1), a_(n,0), a_(n,1), dots, a_(n,n);
    delim: #none,
  )
$ <eq:l1996-enumeration-auxiliary-table>
obtained from $B_(n+1)^+$-matrix [@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990] by
replacing the roots $p_(u v)$ with $a_(u-1,v)$. Denote by $N[(i,v);(k,m)]$ the
number of all steplines from $a_(i v)$ to $a_(k m)$ $(v <= m <= k <= n)$,
$abs(v) <= i <= k$. Lemma~@lem:l1996-enumeration-root-subgroups implies the
following assertion.

#lemma[
  Let $G$ and $K$ be the same as in Lemma~@lem:l1996-enumeration-root-subgroups.
  Then
  $ d(A_(n-1),K) = sum_(i=1)^n N[(i,1);(n,n)], $
  $ d(B_n,K) = sum_(i=0)^n N[(i,-i);(n,n)], $
  $ d(""^2 A_(2n-2),K) = sum_(i=1)^n N[(i,-i+1);(n,n)], $
  $
    d(D_n,K) = d(""^2 A_(2n-2);K) + d(A_(n-1),K) - d(A_(n-2),K)
    + sum_(i=2)^(n-1) sum_(t=i)^(n-1) sum_(m=t+1)^n
    N[(i,-i+1);(t,-1)] dot N[(m,1);(n,n)].
  $
] <lem:l1996-enumeration-stepline-counts>

#source(5, printed: 53)Thus, the problem of evaluation of the numbers $d(G,K)$
under the assumptions of Lemma~@lem:l1996-enumeration-root-subgroups is reduced
to a combinatorial one. In Sections~@sec:l1996-enumeration-combinatorics and
@sec:l1996-enumeration-sums we obtain its solution (as in the case
$G = A_(n-1)$), using integral representation of sums (see
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Egorychev1989]) and listing lattice paths (see
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Feller1967]).

In Lemma~@lem:l1996-enumeration-root-subgroups, the case $2K = 0$, $G = B_n$,
and $C_n$ is essential since $[e_r,e_s] = e_r ast e_s = 2e_(r+s) = 0$ for any
short roots $r$ and $s$ such that $r+s$ is a long root.

Let $T_0$ be a subalgebra of the algebra $N C_n lr((K))$, $2K = 0$, whose basis
is formed by $e_s$ for all possible short roots $s$. It turns out that the
minimal $D$-invariant ideal of the Lie ring $N C_n lr((K))$ containing an
element $e_r$ for arbitrary short root $r$ coincides with the intersection
$T(r) inter T_0$. The latter coincides with the normal $D$-invariant closure of
$e_r$ if $p <= r$ for a long fundamental root $p$ (i.e., $p = p_(1,-1)$,
$r = p_(i,-v)$, $0 < v < i$, in the notation of
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990]). In the case $p ≰ r$, the normal closure
coincides with $T(r)$ because
$ [K e_(i v), K e_(v,-v)] = K e_(i,-v) + K e_(i,-i) quad "for" abs(K) > 2. $
<eq:l1996-enumeration-commutator>
By Theorem~@th:l1982-parabolic-normal-basis-decomposition and
Lemma~@lem:l1982-parabolic-three-blocks of [@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1982],
all $D$-invariant normal subgroups of the adjoint group of $N C_n lr((K))$ are
$K$-linearly generated by elements of the form $e_s$, $s in Phi^+$ (see also
[@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990,
Section~@sec:l1990-chevalley-characteristic-subgroups]), and can be written as
$ sum_(k=1)^m (T_0 inter T(r_k)) + sum_(r in M) K e_r; $
<eq:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-ideal>
here ${r_1,dots,r_m}$ is an arbitrary (possibly empty) set of pairwise
nonincident short roots from $Phi^+$ and $M$ is a subset of long roots from
$Phi^+$. Moreover, if $r in M$ is not a maximal root, then, for some $k$, we
have $r_k <= r$ or $r_k <= r+q$ for any simple root $q$.

Note that the above conditions on $M$ and roots $r_k$ are necessary and
sufficient for a subalgebra @eq:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-ideal to be
an ideal. In particular, if $r in M$ is not a maximal root and $q,r+q in Phi^+$
then $r+q$ is a short root and the inclusion $H ∋ e_(r+q) = e_q ast e_r$ is
equipotent to the inequality $r_k <= r+q$ for some $k$.

#lemma[
  Every normal $D$-invariant subgroup of an adjoint group is an ideal of the Lie
  ring $N C_n lr((K))$, $2K = 0$, $abs(K) > 2$, and can be written in the form
  @eq:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-ideal. The subalgebra
  @eq:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-ideal is a normal subgroup of an
  adjoint group if and only if the following two conditions are satisfied:
  #enum(
    numbering: "(a)",
    [if $r in M$ then all the long roots of height $> h(r)$ are in $M$,],
    [if $p ≰ r_k$, where $1 <= k <= m$, $p = p_(1,-1)$, is a long simple root,
      then every long root $r$ with $r_k <= r$ is in $M$.],
  )
] <lem:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-subgroups>

#proof[
  As was noted above, a $D$-invariant normal subgroup is $K$-linearly generated
  by elements of the form $e_r$, $r in Phi^+$. Moreover, a normal closure of
  $K e_r$ coincides with $T_0 inter T(r)$ for short roots $p <= r$ and with
  $T(r)$ for the rest of them by virtue #source(6, printed: 54)of
  @eq:l1996-enumeration-commutator. Thus, the subgroup is written in the form
  @eq:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-ideal. Conditions (a) and (b) are
  obtained with the help of @eq:l1996-enumeration-commutator.
  Lemma~@lem:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-subgroups is proved.
]

#lemma[
  Let $K$ be a field of characteristic 2 and of order $> 2$. Then
  $ d(C_n,K) = sum_(i=1)^n sum_(t=i)^n (t-i+2) S(i,t,n) $
  <eq:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-count>
  where $S(i,t,n)$ is the number of sets of integer pairs
  $(u_1,v_1),(u_2,v_2),dots,(u_m,v_m)$ such that
  $i = u_1 < u_2 < dots < u_m = n$, $v_1 < v_2 < dots < v_m = n$,
  $-u_k < v_k <= u_k$, and $t$ is the least one among the numbers $u_k$ such
  that the condition $v_k > 0$ is satisfied.
] <lem:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-count>

In Sections~@sec:l1996-enumeration-combinatorics and @sec:l1996-enumeration-sums
it is proved that the right-hand side of
@eq:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-two-count coincides with
$4^(n-1) + binom(2n-1, n)$. For a perfect field of characteristic 2, the groups
$U B_n lr((K))$ and $U C_n lr((K))$ are isomorphic. Consequently,
$d(C_n,K) = d(B_n,K)$. Thus, Theorem~@th:l1996-enumeration-normal-count is
proved.#ed-note[
  The transfer from $C_n$ to $B_n$ here uses the isomorphism over perfect
  fields. This argument does not establish the characteristic-two count for
  $B_n$ over imperfect fields.
]

Theorem~@th:l1996-enumeration-characteristic-count follows from
Theorem~@th:l1996-enumeration-normal-count and the description of characteristic
subgroups (see [@bib:l1996-enumeration-Levchuk1990,
Section~@sec:l1990-chevalley-characteristic-subgroups]).
